  9	
 10	// The delta rule itself: LLM.md L3b, and the one kernel in this model with a
 11	// loop-carried dependency 512 iterations deep.
 12	//
 13	// Per value head and per token, with S the [128, 128] state:
 14	//
 15	//	S  <- S * exp(g)          decay, one scalar for the whole head
 16	//	u  <- v - k^T S           what the state already predicts for this key
 17	//	S  <- S + k (beta*u)^T    write the correction back
 18	//	o  <- q^T S / sqrt(128)   read it out
 19	//
 20	// The state is held **transposed**, s[j*128 + i] = S[i][j], which is the
 21	// reference's layout and not a tidy-up of it: row j is the column all three
 22	// of a token's dot products read, so every access in the loop below is
 23	// contiguous and the row can live in registers across the whole token loop.
 24	// That is what makes the sequential form viable at all.
 25	//
 26	// **The dial is LPC, and it moves three things at once.** A column j of the
 27	// state is owned by LPC lanes holding 128/LPC elements each, so:
 28	//
 29	//	LPC   state/lane   columns per wave   workgroups   q,k re-read   us @ 512
 30	//	 64        2              1              6144         128x         2766
 31	//	 16        8              4              1536          32x          605
 32	//	  8       16              8               768          16x          413  <-
 33	//	  4       32             16               384           8x          454
 34	//	  1      128             64                96           2x          868
 35	//
 36	// Fewer lanes per column is more registers, fewer workgroups (so less
 37	// occupancy), a narrower cross-lane reduction, and — because every workgroup
 38	// of a head reads that head's whole q and k row every token — proportionally
 39	// less traffic. llama.cpp's own kernel sits at the top of that ladder: it
 40	// dispatches {H, n_seqs, S_v} with one column per workgroup, which on a
 41	// 64-wide wave is 6144 workgroups and a 128-fold re-read of q and k.
 42	//
