 44	// rungs are pinned at 1.18-1.31 TB/s while their arithmetic rate varies 4x,
 45	// and deleting the operand loads takes LPC 64 from 2766 us to 1013 where
 46	// deleting both subgroup reductions moves it 5%. Below LPC 8 the register
 47	// file starts costing more occupancy than the saved traffic is worth. The
 48	// measured winner is **413 us a layer against llama.cpp's 438**
 49	// (research/l3b-deltanet-gpu.md).
 50	//
 51	// WAVES is the second dial, and it buys nothing: waves of one workgroup share
 52	// the staged operands, so (LPC, WAVES) = (8, 4) reads each row four times
 53	// instead of sixteen and **ties** the single-wave rung. Once the re-read has
 54	// stopped being the bottleneck, removing more of it is free in the wrong
 55	// direction.
 56	//
 57	// **Where q and k live is the third dial, and its crossover is the same
 58	// rung.** llama.cpp keeps them in registers, 128/LPC of each per lane beside
 59	// the state; at LPC = 1 that would be 384 floats in a register file with 256,
 60	// so this kernel can also stage them once per token in LDS and read them back
 61	// with ordinary indexing (every lane of a cluster reads the same address, a
 62	// broadcast, or LPC consecutive ones; neither conflicts). Registers win 1.15x
 63	// at LPC 16 and 1.08x at LPC 8, and **lose 1.33x at LPC 4**, where 96
 64	// registers a lane costs more occupancy than an LDS round trip costs latency.
 65	// QKREG defaults to registers wherever 128/LPC of each fits, which is every
 66	// rung down to LPC 4; LPC 2 and 1 have no register arm at all.
 67	//
 68	// The head map is **modulo**: value head h reads q/k head h % kvHeads, which
 69	// is `iq1 = iv1 % neq1` in the fused op. It is right only because this
 70	// checkpoint's V heads have already been permuted into tiled order by
 71	// llama.cpp's converter — see llm/deltanet.go, and do not carry it to weights
 72	// read from the HF checkpoint.
